1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,074
2,500 years ago, one empire ruled
almost half the people on Earth.

2
00:00:04,094 --> 00:00:07,878
By Guinness's count, no empire
has ever ruled a bigger share.

3
00:00:08,477 --> 00:00:10,552
Its ceremonial capital stood in Iran.

4
00:00:10,572 --> 00:00:12,553
And you can still climb its stairs.

5
00:00:13,113 --> 00:00:16,546
Those stairs are carved with
delegations from 23 nations,

6
00:00:16,566 --> 00:00:18,444
all bringing gifts to the king.

7
00:00:19,127 --> 00:00:22,217
That is reason number one to visit Iran.

8
00:00:22,237 --> 00:00:26,961
There are 8. A great square. A
New Year over 3,000 years old.

9
00:00:26,981 --> 00:00:28,594
And one place so hot,

10
00:00:28,614 --> 00:00:33,124
a satellite measured 159 degrees
Fahrenheit on its ground.

11
00:00:33,724 --> 00:00:37,402
This is Iran. Close to 90
million people live here.

12
00:00:37,422 --> 00:00:38,997
The capital is Tehran.

13
00:00:39,017 --> 00:00:44,020
And more than 25 of its places are
on the UNESCO World Heritage List.

14
00:00:44,851 --> 00:00:48,411
Reason 1: Persepolis. Darius the
Great began it around 518 BC.

15
00:00:48,431 --> 00:00:50,836
Alexander the Great captured it in 330 BC,

16
00:00:50,856 --> 00:00:53,686
but its stairways and
carved reliefs still stand.

17
00:00:54,350 --> 00:00:57,364
Drive south and you reach Chogha Zanbil.

18
00:00:57,384 --> 00:01:01,006
It is a stepped temple
tower, called a ziggurat,

19
00:01:01,026 --> 00:01:02,599
built around 1250 BC.

20
00:01:02,619 --> 00:01:06,336
That is older than Persepolis
by about 700 years.

21
00:01:06,943 --> 00:01:11,397
Reason 2: Isfahan. Its
great square, Naqsh-e Jahan,

22
00:01:11,417 --> 00:01:15,783
means pattern of the world.
It is 560 meters long,

23
00:01:15,803 --> 00:01:19,312
with two mosques and a palace around it.

24
00:01:19,791 --> 00:01:22,239
It was built between 1598 and 1629. Today,

25
00:01:22,259 --> 00:01:26,314
it is where locals sit on the grass
and have a picnic in the evening.

26
00:01:27,062 --> 00:01:30,771
Reason 3: Shiraz. Walk into the Nasir
al-Mulk Mosque just after sunrise.

27
00:01:30,791 --> 00:01:32,767
Light pours through the stained glass,

28
00:01:32,787 --> 00:01:35,130
and colored patches
slide across the carpets.

29
00:01:35,150 --> 00:01:38,906
Locals call it the Pink
Mosque, for its tiles. Nearby,

30
00:01:38,926 --> 00:01:43,141
the Persian Garden is a UNESCO
listing of 9 gardens in 9 provinces.

31
00:01:43,161 --> 00:01:46,808
Its idea is simple: a shaded
paradise, with running water,

32
00:01:46,828 --> 00:01:48,598
in the middle of a dry land.

33
00:01:49,758 --> 00:01:52,449
Reason 4: Nowruz, the Persian New Year.

34
00:01:52,469 --> 00:01:56,203
It means new day, and it is
more than 3,000 years old.

35
00:01:56,223 --> 00:02:00,186
It starts with the first day
of spring and lasts 13 days.

36
00:02:00,643 --> 00:02:03,591
Families clean the whole house and set a

37
00:02:03,611 --> 00:02:07,079
table with 7 items
starting with the letter S.

38
00:02:07,099 --> 00:02:09,602
Many people jump over small fires.

39
00:02:09,622 --> 00:02:12,961
On day 13, the whole
country picnics outside.

40
00:02:13,460 --> 00:02:15,947
Reason 5: the food. Start with tahdig,

41
00:02:15,967 --> 00:02:19,246
the crispy golden rice
from the bottom of the pot.

42
00:02:19,266 --> 00:02:21,113
The word means exactly that.

43
00:02:21,856 --> 00:02:26,038
Add saffron. Iran grows around
90% of the world's supply.

44
00:02:26,058 --> 00:02:31,587
And for dessert, faloodeh, in Shiraz:
frozen noodles, rose water, and lime.

45
00:02:32,692 --> 00:02:35,206
Reason 6: extremes. Mount Damavand,

46
00:02:35,226 --> 00:02:40,057
a volcano higher than 5,600 meters,
is the tallest volcano in Asia.

47
00:02:40,077 --> 00:02:42,032
And it is capped with snow.

48
00:02:42,699 --> 00:02:47,473
Then the Lut Desert. Its name
means bare, empty land. In 2005,

49
00:02:47,493 --> 00:02:52,345
a NASA satellite measured 159
degrees Fahrenheit on its ground.

50
00:02:52,365 --> 00:02:54,839
That is the ground, not the air.

51
00:02:55,337 --> 00:02:59,876
A later NASA study of 18 years of data
ranked it the hottest surface on Earth,

52
00:02:59,896 --> 00:03:01,591
tied with a desert in Mexico.

53
00:03:02,539 --> 00:03:06,662
Reason 7: Yazd. The whole old
city is built of mud brick,

54
00:03:06,682 --> 00:03:11,438
and people still live in it.
UNESCO listed it in 2017. Look up,

55
00:03:11,458 --> 00:03:16,073
and you will see tall towers that catch the
breeze and pull cool air into the house.

56
00:03:16,093 --> 00:03:19,458
The tallest, at Dowlat Abad
Garden, is almost 34 meters high.

57
00:03:20,439 --> 00:03:23,425
But Yazd gets almost no rain. So
where does its water come from?

58
00:03:23,867 --> 00:03:24,635
From a qanat.

59
00:03:24,655 --> 00:03:29,927
It is a gently sloping tunnel that carries
mountain water for kilometers, with no pump.

60
00:03:30,673 --> 00:03:35,614
The Gonabad qanat has 427 shafts
and over 33 kilometers of tunnel.

61
00:03:35,634 --> 00:03:40,649
It was dug more than 2, 500 years
ago, and it still delivers water.

62
00:03:40,669 --> 00:03:42,473
UNESCO lists 11 of them.

63
00:03:43,481 --> 00:03:45,586
Reason 8: the people. Say salaam.

64
00:03:45,606 --> 00:03:49,063
It means hello, and it comes
from the word for health.

65
00:03:49,083 --> 00:03:53,268
Thank you is mamnoon. Goodbye is
khodahafez, may God protect you.

66
00:03:53,589 --> 00:03:57,806
Dress the way locals do. Women wear
a headscarf and loose clothing.

67
00:03:57,826 --> 00:04:01,177
Men skip the shorts. The
rules apply to tourists too.

68
00:04:04,333 --> 00:04:07,303
Which country should we visit
next? Tell me in the comments.

